#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:4
msgid "guest-memfd support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:6
msgid ""
"Recent kernels allow for the creation of a guest-memfd file descriptor, "
"which can be used to back VMs in a similar manner as a memfd file "
"descriptor, but is intended specifically for this purpose and allows for "
"closer coordination between KVM and the management of this memory to enable "
"more advanced/VM-specific use-cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:12
msgid ""
"Initially this additional functionality centered around providing a common/"
"centralized place for managing kernel-side memory handing requirements for "
"various Confidential Guest architectures. (For more on Confidential Guests, "
"see :ref:`confidential-guest-support`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:17
msgid ""
"guest_memfd has since evolved to become a more general-purpose way to "
"allocate/manage guest memory and potentially allow for things like providing "
"additional memory isolation within the kernel[1] and support for persisting "
"a guest's state across kexec to allow for live-updating the host kernel with "
"minimal guest downtime[2]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:24
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:26
msgid ""
"For Confidential Guests, guest-memfd is currently utilized internally by "
"QEMU to handle private guest memory, independently of whatever memory "
"backend the user has configured for normal/non-private/shared guest memory. "
"To avoid doubling memory, QEMU discards memory in response to the guest "
"converting GPA ranges between shared/private. (e.g. if GPA X is converted "
"from private to shared, the guest-memfd FD offset corresponding to GPA x "
"will be truncated since the memory will be provided by the memory backend "
"the user configured for shared memory, and vice-versa). This is handled "
"automatically/internally for Confidential Guests that rely on this handling "
"and is not directly exposed by QEMU command-line options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:38
msgid ""
"For non-Confidential guests, guest-memfd can be used in a manner that is "
"somewhat interchangeable with a normal memfd. Currently, this is handled by "
"using the same memory-backend implementation as memfd, but with an "
"additional 'guest-memfd=on' option. E.g.::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:43
msgid ""
"qemu ... \\\n"
"  -object memory-backend-memfd,id=ID,size=SIZE,share=on,guest-memfd=on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:46
msgid ""
"Note that the share=on option is required for guest-memfd, since it does not "
"support anonymous memory allocations or COW-like semantics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:49
msgid ""
"Also note that there are a couple of limitations in using guest_memfd in "
"this way compared to a normal memfd, which is why the option is exposed as "
"an experimental for the time being:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:53
msgid "guest_memfd does not currently support the hugetlb=on option"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/guest-memfd.rst:54
msgid "guest_memfd does not currently support Transparent Huge Pages"
msgstr ""

